// XFAIL: RT-393: (RT-393's mechanism, a cycle) `impl A for S where S: B` and `impl B for S where S: A`, neither with type parameters, are never checked: the cycle is accepted, and `need(&S { .. })` under `X: A` runs `a`, which calls `b`, and prints 2 (rustc E0275) (0.30.2)
// From the review of g302oblig-m2 (form fcyc_where2_body). Since v0.30.1
// at least; the release build the same. rustc E0275 (overflow evaluating
// the requirement `S: A`, and `S: B`) at the impls (the rustc twin of this
// file). Siblings: rt_393, rt_393b, rt_393c.
// ERROR: line 20
// ERROR: line 26
trait A {
    fn a(&self) -> i64;
}

trait B {
    fn b(&self) -> i64;
}

struct S {
    r: i64,
}

impl A for S where S: B {
    fn a(&self) -> i64 {
        self.b()
    }
}

impl B for S where S: A {
    fn b(&self) -> i64 {
        2
    }
}

fn need<X: A>(x: &X) -> i64 {
    x.a()
}

fn main() {
    println("{}", need(&S { r: 0 }));
}
